Hermes, the messenger of the gods, carries live stock price updates to Apollo's many dashboard widgets. Hermes maintains a list of registered listeners and notifies each in turn:

```java
public void publish(Price price) {
    for (PriceListener listener : listeners) {
        listener.onPrice(price);
    }
}
```

Which statements about this divine design are true?

Poseidon, ruler of the sea, designs a shared configuration class for his web service that is accessed by every request handler:

```java
public class AppConfig {
    private static AppConfig instance;
    private AppConfig() { }
    public static AppConfig getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new AppConfig();
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

Which criticisms of this design are justified?

The Muse composes a report generator that fixes the order of steps while allowing subclasses to vary two of them:

```java
public abstract class ReportGenerator {
    public final Report generate() {
        Data data = load();
        Data shaped = transform(data);
        return write(render(shaped));
    }
    protected Data load() { return Data.fromDefaultSource(); }
    protected abstract Data transform(Data data);
    protected abstract String render(Data data);
}
```

Which statements about this design are correct?

In the halls of the gods, a scheduling tool sorts appointment dates using interchangeable algorithms.

```java
public interface SortStrategy { void performSort(List<Date> input); }
public class BubbleSort implements SortStrategy { ... }
public class MergeSort  implements SortStrategy { ... }
public class Context { private List<Date> dates; private SortStrategy sortAlgorithm; ... }
public class Policy  { private Context context; public void configure() { ... } }
```

Using the class names from this divine listing: the class that holds the dates and a reference to the currently selected algorithm is [-spot 1]. The type that both `BubbleSort` and `MergeSort` implement is [-spot 2]. The class whose `configure()` method decides which algorithm to select is [-spot 3].

A reviewer examines a notification service in the realm of the gods:

```java
public class OrderService {
    private final SmtpMailer mailer = new SmtpMailer("mail.internal", 25);
    public void placeOrder(Order order) { /* ... */ mailer.send(order.customerEmail(), "..."); }
}
```

The reviewer’s first objection is that `OrderService` names a concrete low‑level class instead of an abstraction such as a `Notifier` interface; the principle being violated is abbreviated [-spot 1] (three letters). The reviewer’s remedy is to stop constructing the collaborator inside the class and supply it from outside instead — the general two‑word name for that technique is [-spot 2].
